  "command": "go test ./internal/metrics -run '^$' -bench '^BenchmarkTrafficUpdates$' -benchmem -cpu=1,2,4,8 -count=3 -benchtime=300ms",
  "method": [
    "All four benchmark processes ran sequentially: baseline, candidate, candidate, baseline.",
    "The first baseline was compiled before editing production metrics.go; the last baseline overlays the unchanged origin/main metrics.go. The benchmark source is identical.",
    "Each RunParallel worker updates one direction per iteration. Duplex assigns alternate workers to inbound/outbound; at GOMAXPROCS=1 it is an inbound-only control.",
    "One update records 16384 bytes and remains immediately visible to Traffic. The operation measures atomic accounting, not moving payload through a tunnel.",
    "Architecture-sized padding uses the existing golang.org/x/sys/cpu dependency. On amd64 the two counters add 256 bytes per process, with no per-connection or per-update allocation."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "These are short microbenchmarks on one Windows host, not Linux/WAN throughput or latency measurements.",
    "Single-direction multi-core contention remains; results vary between runs and do not establish a single-direction speedup.",
    "Lower duplex counter overhead does not translate directly into the same percentage increase in tunnel throughput.",
    "No perf c2c hardware cache-line tracing was performed."
  ],
  "reference": "https://docs.kernel.org/kernel-hacking/false-sharing.html",
